---
// The AP CSP exam reference sheet — the pseudocode students see on exam
// day, with the C# translation they have used all year. Written to match
// the College Board's reference sheet notation as documented in the CED.
import Base from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// pseudocode blocks kept in frontmatter strings so the braces do not trip
// Astro's JSX-like template parser
const CONTROL_BLOCK = `IF (condition)
{
    &lt;block of statements&gt;
}
ELSE
{
    &lt;block of statements&gt;
}

REPEAT n TIMES
{
    &lt;block of statements&gt;
}

REPEAT UNTIL (condition)
{
    &lt;block of statements&gt;
}

FOR EACH item IN aList
{
    &lt;block of statements&gt;
}`;

const PROC_BLOCK = `PROCEDURE name (parameter1, parameter2)
{
    &lt;block of statements&gt;
    RETURN(expression)
}`;

const DRILL2 = `x ← RANDOM(1, 6)
REMOVE(deck, 1)
FOR EACH c IN deck`;

const LIST_INIT = 'new List&lt;int&gt; { value1, value2, value3 }';
---
<Base title="Exam Reference Sheet — AP CS Principles" active="exam" root=".." docsHome="../docs/index.html" css="../style.css"
  description="The AP CSP exam reference sheet pseudocode, side by side with the C# and Unity code this course uses all year.">
<h1>Exam Reference Sheet</h1>

<p>
    On exam day you get a one-page reference sheet of pseudocode. You have
    written the C# version of all of it for a year — the only new skill is
    reading their exact notation. Here is the full sheet, side by side with
    what it looks like in our world.
</p>

<h2>Displaying and assignment</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Reference sheet</th><th>What it means</th><th>Our C#</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>DISPLAY(expression)</code></td><td>Output the value</td><td><code>Debug.Log(expression)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>a ← expression</code></td><td>Assign (the arrow, not =)</td><td><code>a = expression;</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>RANDOM(a, b)</code></td><td>A random integer from a to b, inclusive</td><td><code>Random.Range(a, b + 1)</code> (Unity's is exclusive at the top for ints)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Arithmetic and logic</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Reference sheet</th><th>What it means</th><th>Our C#</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>a MOD b</code></td><td>Remainder after dividing</td><td><code>a % b</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>a / b</code></td><td>Integer division truncates when both are integers</td><td><code>a / b</code> (same rule in C#!)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NOT, AND, OR</code></td><td>Boolean operators</td><td><code>!, &amp;&amp;, ||</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>=, ≠, &gt;, &lt;, ≥, ≤</code></td><td>Comparisons</td><td><code>==, !=, &gt;, &lt;, &gt;=, &lt;=</code></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Selection and iteration</h2>

<pre><code set:html={CONTROL_BLOCK} /></pre>

<p>
    Note what the sheet does NOT have: <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)</code>
    — the exam counts loops with REPEAT n TIMES and walks lists with FOR
    EACH. A counted loop becomes "REPEAT n TIMES" plus your own counter
    if you need the index.
</p>

<h2>Lists (the exam's biggest topic)</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Reference sheet</th><th>What it means</th><th>Our C#</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>aList ← [value1, value2, value3]</code></td><td>Create a list with those values</td><td><code set:html={LIST_INIT} /></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>aList[i]</code></td><td>The value at position i — <strong>starts at 1, not 0</strong></td><td><code>aList[i - 1]</code> (C# indexes from 0!)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>INSERT(aList, i, value)</code></td><td>Put value at position i, shifting the rest</td><td><code>aList.Insert(i - 1, value)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>APPEND(aList, value)</code></td><td>Add value at the end</td><td><code>aList.Add(value)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>REMOVE(aList, i)</code></td><td>Remove the value at position i</td><td><code>aList.RemoveAt(i - 1)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>LENGTH(aList)</code></td><td>How many values</td><td><code>aList.Count</code></td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout trap">
    <strong>The #1 exam trap:</strong> the reference sheet's lists are
    <strong>1-indexed</strong>. Your C# is 0-indexed. When a question says
    "REMOVE(aList, 1)", cross out index 0 in your head. This one
    mistranslation misses more questions than any other mistake on the
    exam — count on your fingers every single time.
</div>

<h2>Procedures</h2>

<pre><code set:html={PROC_BLOCK} /></pre>

<p>
    A procedure is our method; a parameter is the variable it receives;
    RETURN hands a value back to whoever called it. You built these all
    year: <code>ChangeHealth(int amount)</code>,
    <code>ChangeCoins(int amount)</code>, <code>Shuffle(List&lt;Card_data&gt; deck)</code>.
</p>

<h2>Reading-trap drills</h2>

<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Clinic drill</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Drill 1:</strong> Write your card-jam Shuffle in exam
    pseudocode. Then hand it to a neighbor and have THEM trace what it
    displays on a 5-card deck.</p>
    <p><strong>Drill 2:</strong> Convert these exam lines to C#:
    <code set:html={DRILL2} />. The first one surprises people.</p>
    <p><strong>Drill 3:</strong> Trace <code>REPEAT UNTIL (LENGTH(aList) = 1)</code>
    starting with a 4-item list where each pass removes item 2. How many
    repetitions? Count them, do not estimate.</p>
</div>
</Base>
